        {
          "id": "line-102",
          "kind": "heading",
          "text": "#### func AfterFunc ¶ added in go1.21.0\n",
          "parent_id": "line-100",
          "start_line": 102,
          "end_line": 102
        },
        {
          "id": "line-112",
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "text": "Calling the returned stop function stops the association of ctx with f. It returns true if the call stopped f from being run. If stop returns false, either the context is canceled and f has been started in its own goroutine; or f was already stopped. The stop function does not wait for f to complete before returning. If the caller needs to know whether f is completed, it must coordinate with f explicitly.\n",
          "parent_id": "line-102",
          "start_line": 112,
          "end_line": 112
        },
        {
          "id": "line-519",
          "kind": "heading",
          "text": "### Types ¶\n",
          "parent_id": "line-1",
          "start_line": 519,
          "end_line": 519
        },
        {
          "id": "line-535",
          "kind": "heading",
          "text": "#### type CancelFunc ¶\n",
          "parent_id": "line-519",
          "start_line": 535,
          "end_line": 535
        },
        {
          "id": "line-537",
          "kind": "code",
          "text": "```\ntype CancelFunc func()\n```\n",
          "parent_id": "line-535",
          "start_line": 537,
          "end_line": 539
        },
        {
          "id": "line-541",
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "text": "A CancelFunc tells an operation to abandon its work. A CancelFunc does not wait for the work to stop. A CancelFunc may be called by multiple goroutines simultaneously. After the first call, subsequent calls to a CancelFunc do nothing.\n",
          "parent_id": "line-535",
          "start_line": 541,
          "end_line": 541
        }
      ],
